@prefix plan: <../plan-ontology.ttl#>.
@prefix dct: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/>.
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#>.

<plan.ttl#plan> a plan:Plan;
    dct:title "RichEditor mentions <- full-text search (refactor first)";
    dct:description "Replace hard-coded items in RichEditor markdown mentions with label search over the search index, same as pos-navigation. Refactor the search-index ownership first, re-enable the mention extension afterwards.";
    plan:status plan:in-progress;
    dct:created "2026-10-09"^^xsd:date;
    plan:hasTask <./mentions-fulltext-search.ttl#task-01-cache-search-index-in-core>, <./mentions-fulltext-search.ttl#task-02-recreate-search-index-on-label-update>;
    plan:context "Human topic (verbatim) [H]: \"instead of hard-coded items in RichEditor markdown mentions, i want to have a full text search from the search index, same as in pos-navigation\".", "Terminology: 'full text search' is a mislabel — the index covers labels and URIs only, not document content. The mentions feature wants label search over findable things. Keeping scope as label search. [LLM, H implicitly]", "User framing (verbatim) [H]: \"For sure we need a refactoring first and we should plan this now before diving deeper into the mentions feature. the question is where to build the search index and whether we need to 'globalize' it\".";
    plan:decision "Move search into core. [H] User: \"I think it makes sense to move it into core, since it is not a UI feature? even cli apps could make use of the search?\"", "Core owns lifecycle (option A-full, LLM inference from the user's core argument + headless-first choice): PodOS holds a SearchIndexService, built lazily when first consumer expresses need, shared thereafter; cleared on logout; updated internally by addToLabelIndex / createDefaultLabelIndex.", "Build lazily on demand, not eagerly on login. User: \"i think it should be lazy, but not on first search, but as soon as a component needs it. like pos-navigation or the mention\". Demand signal: any consumer mount [H].", "Keep warm until logout. User: \"keep warm.\" [LLM] Invalidation on label change is an existing mechanism: pos-make-findable writes via core (os.addToLabelIndex / os.createDefaultLabelIndex) and only then echoes DOM events which pos-navigation consumes. With core-owned index, core rebuilds inside those gateway methods; DOM-event listeners become redundant.", "Keep os.buildSearchIndex(profile) as the explicit demand call [H]. User: \"why should we remove os.buildSearchIndex, i would still keep it explicit\". New semantics [LLM]: de-duplicated ensure-build returning the shared instance; keyed on session profile; same-profile repeat calls are no-ops; profile change (re-login) rebuilds.", "Core rebuilds internally on label changes [H] (user confirmed): addToLabelIndex / createDefaultLabelIndex update the core-held index directly; DOM-event listeners in pos-navigation become redundant.", "Remove pod-os:search:index-created/updated DOM events [H] (user confirmed, breaking but contained — only in-repo consumers).", "Defer os-to-RichEditor bridge and mention fallback [H] until after the refactor. User: \"focus on refactoring, we will discuss after that\" / \"later\".", "Headless-first design [H]: core API must work without DOM; CLI/non-UI consumers usable from day one.", "Scope guardrail [H]: \"lets not add new things and focus on refactoring\" — pure deduplication/refactor of existing behavior, no new features.", "Task-breakdown convention [H, clarified 2026-10-09]: plan = what (slice order/roadmap, plan-level decisions, plan-blocking open questions, task status); task files = how (code facts, slice-level scope, design sketches, test plans, slice-local open questions). LLM rule: before writing to plan ask 'does this change what we plan, or only how a planned item is done?' Only the latter goes to the task level.", "Merge strategy for feat/markdown-mentions [H]: disable the mention extension before merging (user: \"i would like to remove the mention extension from the editor. tests might break. i have no better idea to skip those then until the extension is back in\" / \"disable ext. skip affectedf tests\" / \"Skip via .skip\"). Unregister mention() in RichEditor.ts on the branch; the 3 registration-tied tests in RichEditor.spec.tsx get .skip treatment (user chose .skip over delete-and-restore); standalone tests keep running; mention rendering/serialization code (PosRichLinkMark, markdown syntax) stays on main inert; re-add path: when the search-based provider lands, the extension is re-registered and the 3 skipped tests un-skipped. Applied [LLM]: disable commit made on feat/markdown-mentions, RichEditor suite green (39 passed / 3 skipped / 0 failed), LSP diagnostics clean; pending: commit plan-file move, then merge tip to main.";
    plan:option "A. Core-owned search service [LLM]: PodOS holds a SearchIndex instance; lifecycle built on login, invalidated/rebuilt on label-index change events, cleared on logout; consumers call os.search(term) synchronously. + lunr and LabelIndex logic already live in core; single source of truth; one fetch, one rebuild. - core must know about session/login lifecycle or get an explicit initSearch(profile) call; introduces stateful lifetime into an otherwise stateless-ish facade; elements must still bridge os-to-RichEditor (RichEditor has no os reference; needs a suggestionsProvider callback injected at construction).", "B. Shared service in elements package [LLM]: a tiny module in elements/src owns index lifecycle (subscribes session + index events once), exposes an observable / query function. Core API stays as-is. + core stays thin; consumers live in elements anyway; no core API surface change. - singleton module is hidden global state; harder to test; two search entry points (os.buildSearchIndex still exists) can drift.", "C. Status quo pattern (per-component build) [LLM]: pos-markdown-document subscribes pod-os:init + session like pos-navigation and builds its own index. + least refactoring, proven pattern, testable in isolation. - N times label-index fetches and lunr rebuilds; staleness wiring duplicated per consumer; RichEditor still needs the bridge; with a third consumer this clearly breaks down.";
    plan:note "Middle path [LLM]: lifecycle correctness (one owner of build/rebuild/clear, others just query) matters more than where the instance lives; both A and B can satisfy it; A puts the truth in core, B in elements.", "Exposure analysis for the merge decision [LLM]: the hardcoded suggestion menu (Alice/Bob/Carol on localhost:3000) only appears when a user with edit permission enters edit mode and types '#'; view mode and stored mention markdown ([@ id=... label=...] rendered as pos-rich-link) don't touch items.ts. But elements is published and pos-document/pos-app-document-viewer pass editable through — downstream editors would see fake suggestions and could insert broken URIs.";
    plan:openQuestion "os-to-RichEditor bridge design — deferred to post-refactor discussion [H].", "Mention fallback for things not in the index — deferred [H].", "Naming of core search service class — TBD during implementation planning.".
<./mentions-fulltext-search.ttl#task-01-cache-search-index-in-core> a plan:Task;
    dct:title "Cache SearchIndex in core's buildSearchIndex";
    plan:parent <plan.ttl#plan>;
    plan:status plan:done;
    plan:touchesFile "core/src/search/SearchGateway.ts", "core/src/search/SearchGateway.spec.ts";
    plan:goal "Repeated calls to os.buildSearchIndex(profile) with the same profile return the same cached SearchIndex instance instead of fetching label indexes and rebuilding every time.";
    plan:scope "Cache only [H]: no logout-clear, no DOM-event changes, no result-shape change.", "Same profile -> no-op; different profile -> rebuild. 'Same profile' means comparing profile.webId (not object identity).", "No in-flight dedup: concurrent first calls may each build; last writer wins. Accepted wart.", "Test security [LLM]: new core spec pinning caching semantics; pos-navigation suite stays green. Wallaby runs are executed on the user's side (LLM sandbox cannot run wallaby).";
    plan:verification "[LLM from H decisions] New unit spec: same webId twice -> same SearchIndex instance (toBe), one fetchAll call; different webId -> new instance, second fetch; empty-profile path -> empty SearchIndex, cached (same instance on repeat).";
    plan:outOfScope "Clearing cached index on logout -> creates a known stale cross-user index window (re-login as another user serves the previous user's label index until a rebuild is triggered). Flagged as privacy-adjacent; superseded by task-02's plain-cache + logout-clear.", "Internal rebuild on addToLabelIndex / createDefaultLabelIndex (task-02).", "Removal of pod-os:search:index-created/updated DOM events (tasks 03/04).", "Result shape {ref} -> {uri, label}.", "os-to-RichEditor bridge, mention fallback.";
    plan:decision "Cache placement resolved [H]: inside SearchGateway (it already owns build logic).", "Event-triggered rebuild bypassed by cache is not an issue [H]: SearchIndex.rebuild() never goes through os.buildSearchIndex; pos-navigation's rebuildSearchIndex() calls SearchIndex.rebuild() directly, re-reading label indexes from the store in place.", "Stale cross-user index on re-login is not an issue [H]: the cache is keyed by webId, so login as a different user is a cache miss and triggers a fresh build; pos-navigation additionally clears its index on logout.", "Unit tests suffice for the cache [H, 2026-10-09]. Angelo challenged integration tests: \"why do we need integration tests, i think unit would be enough for the caching feature\". Caching semantics (memoization by webId) are pure logic, tested unit-style in SearchGateway.spec.ts with a fake Store. The duplicated describe(\"caching\") block was removed from SearchGateway.integration.spec.ts; the original build-search-index integration tests remain (they cover fetch -> parse -> LabelIndex -> SearchIndex wiring on first build). Accepted trade-off: no test asserts that the memoized index still searches correctly (wiring is covered by the non-cached integration tests). The extracted pure cache class (SearchIndexCache) was NOT taken — fake-store unit tests on SearchGateway are sufficient. [H]", "Implementation review (Angelo, 2026-10-09) [H]: the duplicated cache-write/return in the two branches was consolidated; the let assignment was replaced by a private buildIndex() helper with an early return for the empty-profile path.";
    plan:note "Task superseded in parts by task-02 (plain cache, no webId keying; logout-clear added there). Open questions from this task were all resolved.".
<./mentions-fulltext-search.ttl#task-02-recreate-search-index-on-label-update> a plan:Task;
    dct:title "Update cached search index on label-index writes";
    plan:parent <plan.ttl#plan>;
    plan:status plan:done;
    dct:created "2026-10-10"^^xsd:date;
    plan:touchesFile "core/src/search/SearchGateway.ts", "core/src/search/SearchGateway.spec.ts", "core/src/search/createDefaultLabelIndex.ts", "core/src/search/addToLabelIndex.ts";
    plan:goal "With a cache present [LLM, from post-task-01 discussion], addToLabelIndex rebuilds the cached index in place and createDefaultLabelIndex recreates it, so core owns index freshness. Both updates are awaited before the method resolves — callers get a freshness guarantee (unlike the pre-01 event timing, which was racy).";
    plan:context "Code facts [LLM]: Store.get(uri) returns a fresh wrapper but reads live from the shared rdflib store; LabelIndex.getIndexedItems() re-reads the store, so SearchIndex.rebuild() sees statements written after build time — as long as the label-index document set is unchanged. SearchIndex privately holds the LabelIndex[] captured at construction. rebuild() is called in exactly two places today: the SearchIndex constructor and pos-navigation's pod-os:search:index-updated listener. Pre-01 semantics: the index-updated path never refetched (shared store already had the new triples); the index-created path called buildSearchIndex, which refetched all label indexes.", "Regression from task-01 (commit 47c1360a) [LLM, user confirmed]: the create-default-index flow broke — post-01 the buildSearchIndex call is a cache hit (same webId) so a stale instance is returned, and a rebuild over the captured (possibly empty) LabelIndex[] never includes the new document. User named the underlying issue: the cached SearchIndex contains a list of all label indexes, so a new index document requires more than rebuild() — it requires building the cached index anew; rebuild() naming is confusing for that.", "Ordering challenge [LLM]: if the real goal is re-enabling the mention extension (disabled for the merge), the shortest path is result-shape + bridge with clean-up afterwards; if the goal is 'core is the single source of search truth first', this task comes first. Plan leans refactoring-first; user chose this task [H].";
    plan:decision "Plain cache, no webId keying [H]. User challenged the webId key from task-01: \"why do we need the cache by web id at all? only one user is logged in at a time and the cache needs to be cleared on logout\". The gateway holds one optional SearchIndex; the {webId, index} tuple and its comparison logic are removed (small refactor of task-01's cache, no new functionality).", "Cache cleared on logout [H] — follows from the same quote; implementation goes through core's existing session subscription. Task-01's resolved open question ('cross-user staleness handled by webId keying') is thereby superseded.", "No cache -> no-op on write paths [H]: \"if nothing cached nothing needs to be rebuilt -> noop\". Write paths never build an index.", "Method-per-operation [H]. User challenged the discriminator options: \"why so complicated. recreation right now is only when default index is created\". addToLabelIndex -> cache exists -> rebuild() (document set unchanged; captured LabelIndex objects read the new triples live from the store). createDefaultLabelIndex -> cache exists -> recreate: build a fresh SearchIndex from the current profile.getPrivateLabelIndexes() (the solid:privateLabelIndex triple is already in the local rdflib store when the subsequent add runs). Supersedes the earlier interim decision 'only addToLabelIndex touches the cache'.", "Pure refactor, no new functionality [LLM]: the moved trigger is the existing pos-navigation index-updated rebuild; recreation restores pre-01 index-created refetch semantics in deterministic form (bug fix, not new behavior). Core-only slice: pos-navigation untouched; its index-updated listener becomes a harmless duplicate rebuild of the same object.";
    plan:verification "[LLM] Unit tests (fake Store, style of task-01): buildSearchIndex twice -> same instance; after logout-simulation (cache cleared) -> next call builds again. addToLabelIndex with no cache -> store write happens, fetchAll not called, no rebuild. addToLabelIndex with cache -> same instance (toBe), search() finds the added item, no refetch of label indexes. createDefaultLabelIndex with cache -> cache is a new instance, search() finds an item added to the new index afterwards (via subsequent addToLabelIndex in the same test). createDefaultLabelIndex with no cache -> no build. Awaiting: promise from addToLabelIndex resolves only after the cache update. Logout-clear: unit-testable via the session subject, covered at the level the existing flagAuthorizationMetadata tap is covered.", "Wallaby run (user side): core suite + pos-navigation suite green.";
    plan:scope "SearchGateway: replace private cached? {webId, index} with private cached? SearchIndex; buildSearchIndex(profile) drops the webId comparison — cache exists -> return it, else build (empty-profile path still caches an empty index).", "SearchGateway.addToLabelIndex(thing, labelIndex): await store.executeUpdate(...), then if (this.cached) { this.cached.rebuild(); }.", "SearchGateway.createDefaultLabelIndex(profile): after executeUpdate and returning the LabelIndex, if this.cached exists -> recreate: this.cached = await this.buildIndex(profile.getPrivateLabelIndexes()) — refetches the label-index documents (including the new one) and constructs a fresh SearchIndex (reuses the existing private buildIndex helper).", "PodOS: in the session subscription (alongside flagAuthorizationMetadata()), clear the gateway's cache when the session ends.";
    plan:note "Open question 1 (webId scoping/discriminator) dissolved [H] by the plain-cache + method-per-operation decisions.", "Open question 2 (session-clear hook shape), resolved direction: the existing flagAuthorizationMetaDataOnSessionChange tap could be used 'but should probably be renamed' since it would then do more than flag authorization metadata; proposal for the logout-clear slice: rename to handleSessionChange(), body delegates to named private reactions (store.flagAuthorizationMetadata(), later searchGateway.clear()). Exact naming decided when that slice is planned.", "Open question 3 (DOM-event removal): decided [H, 2026-10-09] — 'the breaking change is still acceptable'; remove pod-os:search:index-created/updated in a slice after this task. Analysis: the deprecate-while-emitting alternative seems moot given core owns index freshness after this task.", "Open question 4 (filesToCreate: [] in createDefaultLabelIndex): the returned operation lists no files to create even though the target file may not exist yet (presumably the solid server creates it on PUT — to verify). Does not affect gateway logic; noted.", "Open question 5 (logout-clear timing vs in-flight build) [LLM]: if buildSearchIndex is mid-flight during logout, the clear must not be overwritten by the completing build (task-01 accepted 'no in-flight dedup'; the clear adds a stale-write race). Accepted as-is [H, 2026-10-10]: wart stands, no code change.", "Post-implementation review [LLM]: two-axis LLM review ran 2026-10-10; findings triaged and not acted on [H]. The profile-staleness concern was retracted on re-verification (getPrivateLabelIndexes() reads live from the rdflib store and createDefaultLabelIndex's update operations already insert the solid:privateLabelIndex triple before the recreate runs); the remaining findings were test-assertion nits and style notes. Implemented via commits e82ee88a..9c14f84b.".

